---
import CalculatorPage from '../components/CalculatorPage.astro';
import EmailEngagementCalculator from '../components/EmailEngagementCalculator.astro';
const faqs = [
  { q:'How do I calculate click-to-open rate?', a:'Divide unique recipients who clicked by unique recipients with tracked opens, then multiply by 100. With 1,250 click recipients and 20,000 open recipients, CTOR is 6.25%. Use counts from the same campaign and tracking settings.' },
  { q:'What is the difference between email CTR and CTOR?', a:'Email CTR divides unique click recipients by delivered emails. CTOR divides those click recipients by tracked open recipients. With 50,000 deliveries, 20,000 opens and 1,250 click recipients, email CTR is 2.5% and CTOR is 6.25%; one rate cannot be substituted for the other.' },
  { q:'Should I use total opens or unique opens?', a:'Use unique recipients for both opens and clicks. One recipient opening ten times still counts once in this model. Total events and unique recipients measure different things, so mixing them produces misleading percentages.' },
  { q:'Why is a rate undefined when its denominator is zero?', a:'Division by zero does not produce a valid rate. With no tracked opens, CTOR is undefined even if click activity exists. With no delivered emails, open rate and email CTR are undefined; the calculator shows this explicitly rather than reporting 0%.' },
  { q:'Why can clicks exceed tracked opens?', a:'Open tracking and click tracking can record different activity. Missing tracking images, privacy protections and different bot filters can make the counts inconsistent. This calculator flags clicks above tracked opens so the arithmetic is not mistaken for a trustworthy engagement comparison.' },
  { q:'What is a good email engagement rate?', a:'Compare campaigns with the same audience, message purpose, recipient definitions and filtering rules. A broad industry average is not a target for every campaign, and privacy-related opens can distort comparisons. Review downstream conversions and contribution alongside recorded email engagement.' },
];
---
<CalculatorPage slug="email-engagement-calculator" title="Email Engagement Calculator: Open Rate, CTR & CTOR" description="Email engagement calculator for open rate, email CTR and click-to-open rate. Use unique recipients, compare denominators and handle missing tracked opens." name="Email Engagement Calculator" intro="Calculate open rate, email click-through rate and click-to-open rate from one campaign. Unique recipient counts keep the denominators clear; zero denominators remain undefined." badges={['Three rates together','Unique recipient counts','No signup']} features={['Open rate','Email CTR','Click-to-open rate','Undefined denominator handling']} faqs={faqs}>
  <div class="definition-box"><p><strong>Click-to-open rate (CTOR)</strong> is unique click recipients divided by unique tracked open recipients, multiplied by 100. Email CTR uses delivered emails as its denominator. Use the <a href="/email-marketing-roi-calculator/">email ROI calculator</a> to connect engagement assumptions with costs and revenue.</p></div>
  <EmailEngagementCalculator />
  <section class="content-section" aria-labelledby="email-formulas"><h2 id="email-formulas">Email Open Rate, CTR and CTOR Formulas</h2>
    <p>Each rate answers a different question. Keep one campaign, reporting window and unique-recipient definition across all three inputs.</p>
    <div class="bench-table-wrap"><table class="bench-table"><thead><tr><th>Rate</th><th>Formula</th><th>Denominator</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Open rate</td><td>Unique open recipients ÷ delivered emails × 100</td><td>Successfully delivered emails</td></tr><tr><td>Email CTR</td><td>Unique click recipients ÷ delivered emails × 100</td><td>Successfully delivered emails</td></tr><tr><td>CTOR</td><td>Unique click recipients ÷ tracked open recipients × 100</td><td>Recipients with tracked opens</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
    <p>The <a href="/ctr-calculator/">ad and search CTR calculator</a> uses impressions instead of email deliveries. Label the denominator whenever sharing a click-through rate.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="content-section" aria-labelledby="email-example"><h2 id="email-example">Worked Example: One Email Campaign</h2>
    <p>For an illustrative campaign with 50,000 delivered emails, 20,000 unique open recipients and 1,250 unique click recipients, open rate is 40%, email CTR is 2.5% and CTOR is 6.25%.</p>
    <div class="formula-box"><p>Open rate: 20,000 ÷ 50,000 × 100 = <strong>40%</strong><br />Email CTR: 1,250 ÷ 50,000 × 100 = <strong>2.5%</strong><br />CTOR: 1,250 ÷ 20,000 × 100 = <strong>6.25%</strong></p></div>
    <p>These are original example inputs, rather than industry benchmarks. If 100 of those 1,250 click recipients purchase, the click-to-purchase conversion rate is 8%; it is a separate metric with a separate outcome. The <a href="/conversion-rate-calculator/">conversion rate calculator</a> can calculate that step.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="content-section" aria-labelledby="email-tracking"><h2 id="email-tracking">Read Engagement With Its Tracking Limits</h2>
    <p>Recorded opens are not a direct count of people reading a message. Image loading, privacy protections and automated activity can change what your platform records; use consistent filtering before comparing campaigns.</p>
    <p><a href="https://mailchimp.com/help/about-open-and-click-rates/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Mailchimp's open and click rate documentation</a> explains image-based open tracking and why bot filtering matters. <a href="https://knowledge.hubspot.com/marketing-email/analyze-your-marketing-email-campaign-performance" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">HubSpot's email performance documentation</a> also distinguishes click rate from click-through rate based on opens. Vendor labels vary, so inspect the underlying formula.</p>
    <p>This tool accepts clicks above tracked opens and flags the mismatch; a displayed CTOR above 100% indicates incompatible tracking counts, not superior engagement. Counts above deliveries are rejected because the inputs must represent unique recipients for one campaign.</p>
    <p>See the <a href="/blog/email-marketing-roi-benchmarks/">email ROI and measurement guide</a> for the relationship between engagement assumptions and commercial returns. Definitions and original arithmetic reviewed October 22, 2026.</p>
  </section>
  <div class="related-box"><p class="related-box__label">Continue Your Email Analysis</p><div class="link-grid"><a class="link-tile" href="/email-marketing-roi-calculator/">Email ROI Calculator →</a><a class="link-tile" href="/ctr-calculator/">CTR Calculator →</a><a class="link-tile" href="/conversion-rate-calculator/">Conversion Rate Calculator →</a><a class="link-tile" href="/blog/email-marketing-roi-benchmarks/">Email ROI Guide →</a></div></div>
</CalculatorPage>
